e('Trust flows down: if you trust the root\'s key, and the root\'s signature on the intermediate verifies, you can trust the intermediate\'s key, and so on to the leaf.','Trust follows a **valid certification path**, not signatures alone: validity, constraints, permitted uses and relying-party policy must also pass.')
e('A root key is almost impossible to replace, because it is baked into billions of devices.','Replacing a widely distributed trust anchor can be operationally difficult because many relying parties need updated trust stores.','Avoiduniversaldevicecount')
e('limiting how many CAs may sit below this one.','limiting the number of non-self-issued intermediate CA certificates below it in a valid path (not leaf certificates).','pathLenprecisecount')
e('**SCT list**: proof the certificate was logged in Certificate Transparency (below).','**SCT list**: signed promises of timely inclusion in Certificate Transparency logs; an SCT is not itself a Merkle inclusion proof.','SCTnotinclusionproof',[ct])
e('Every field in the checklist exists because someone once skipped it.','Microsoft documented the missing Basic Constraints checks in MS02-050; path authorization is separate from signature validity.','Avoidunsupportedcausalhistory',['https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security-updates/securitybulletins/2002/ms02-050'])
e('The private key never leaves your server (or HSM).','Protect the private key; where it is generated, backed up or used depends on the deployment and key-management service.','Notallprivatekeysnonexportable')
e('and today it is almost always automated with **ACME** (RFC 8555), the protocol Let\'s Encrypt created.','and **ACME** (RFC 8555) is one widely used automation protocol. It was developed through the IETF, with Let\'s Encrypt as an early deployment.','AvoidunmeasuredACMEprevalence',[a])
e('| HTTP-01 | Serve token at `/.well-known/acme-challenge/` |','| HTTP-01 | Serve key authorization under `/.well-known/acme-challenge/<token>` |','Tokenboundaccountkey',[a])
