e('Wildcard certificates require DNS-01, since controlling one web server does not prove you control a whole zone.','For Let\'s Encrypt wildcard requests, use DNS-01. Control of the delegated challenge record can satisfy this validation; it need not give control of every record in the entire zone.','ScopeCApolicyanddelegation',[le])
e('Every certificate is inside its validity window.','The certificates in the path are within their validity windows. A trust anchor is an input to validation, not necessarily processed as another path certificate; root-certificate expiry handling varies.','Trustanchornotordinarypathcert')
e('Every issuer has `CA:TRUE`, `keyCertSign` and any path length or name constraints are respected.','Intermediate issuers are authorized CAs; Basic Constraints, applicable Key Usage, path length, name constraints and unrecognized critical extensions are checked.','KeyUsageconditionalRFCandcriticalext')
e('Nothing in the chain is revoked, and (in browsers) CT proofs are present.','Apply the client\'s revocation and browser CT policies. Coverage, freshness and failure handling vary; certificate-path validation alone does not establish either.','PathvalidationnotrevocationCT')
e('It checks signatures only, not dates, names, constraints or revocation. Never write your own validator for production. Use the platform\'s, or `cryptography.x509.verification.PolicyBuilder`, which does all five steps and rejects a leaf whose SAN does not match.','`verify_directly_issued_by` checks issuer/subject name agreement and signature validity; this loop does not check dates, hostname, CA constraints, trust, revocation or CT. Use a maintained validator and configure the required policy. `cryptography.x509.verification.PolicyBuilder` can build a server verifier with hostname/path checks, but does **not** automatically perform live revocation or browser CT enforcement.','PolicyBuilderdoesnotallfive',['https://cryptography.io/en/latest/x509/verification/','https://cryptography.io/en/latest/x509/reference/'])
e('So most clients **soft-fail** and accept the certificate, which is exactly what an attacker who can block traffic wants. Adam Langley famously compared soft-fail revocation to a seatbelt that snaps when you crash.','A **soft-fail** policy accepts when status cannot be obtained, which an attacker may exploit by blocking retrieval. Other clients use hard-fail, cached or pushed status. No single behavior applies to every browser, platform and private PKI.','Removeunsupportedmajorityandquote')
e('Browsers therefore stopped relying on live checks. Chrome pushes a curated **CRLSet** of important revocations; Firefox\'s **CRLite** compresses every revoked web certificate into a compact filter that is downloaded in the background.','Browser approaches differ. Chrome distributes **CRLSets** with selected revocations rather than complete coverage. Mozilla began production **CRLite** use in Firefox 142, using compressed revocation data for covered WebPKI certificates; coverage and fallback policy matter.','Versionandcoverage',['https://www.chromium.org/Home/chromium-security/crlsets/','https://hacks.mozilla.org/2025/08/crlite-fast-private-and-comprehensive-certificate-revocation-checking-in-firefox/'])
e('The deeper fix is to make certificates expire before revocation matters.','Shorter validity reduces the exposure window for a particular certificate, but does not eliminate the need for revocation, key replacement or preventing fraudulent renewal.','Shortlife notrevocationreplacement')
e('With 47-day certificates, renewal must be automated, so ACME stops being optional.','The issuance-date cutoffs are 15 March 2026, 2027 and 2029 respectively. Automation becomes operationally valuable; the policy does not mandate ACME specifically.','ExactcutoffandnotACMEmandate',[ca])
e('Any of the roughly 150 trusted roots can issue a certificate for *any* domain. One bad CA undermines everyone.','An unconstrained trusted CA can authorize names outside the domains it usually serves. The accepted root set and constraints vary by platform and policy, so one compromised CA can affect many relying parties.','Unsupportedrootcountanduniversalnamespace')
e('Browsers removed DigiNotar\'s roots within days and the company went bankrupt within weeks.','Mozilla removed trust following the incident. This example concerns the documented certificate compromise, not a verified account of the company\'s later legal or financial proceedings.','Removeunverifiedbankruptcytimeline',['https://blog.mozilla.org/security/2011/09/02/diginotar-removal-follow-up/'])
e('which goes into the certificate itself.','which can be delivered in a certificate extension or by other supported TLS/OCSP mechanisms.','SCTdeliverynotonlycert',[ct])
e('Repeated failures cost Symantec its trust in 2018 and Entrust in 2024: browsers stopped trusting their newly issued certificates.','Chrome\'s 2018 Symantec changes eventually distrusted certificates chaining to its old infrastructure, not only newly issued ones. Its Entrust/AffirmTrust change used a November 2024 issuance cutoff. Exact browser policies and managed-environment exceptions differ.','Distinguishdistrustscope',['https://security.googleblog.com/2017/09/chromes-plan-to-distrust-symantec.html','https://security.googleblog.com/2024/06/sustaining-digital-certificate-security.html'])
e('Use name constraints on internal intermediates so a leak cannot mint certificates for public domains.','Appropriate name constraints can limit what enforcing clients accept, but must cover the identity forms actually used. DNS constraints alone do not constrain arbitrary SPIFFE URI identities. A stolen issuing key can keep signing until its trust is removed or otherwise rejected.','Constraintsnameformandcompromiseconditions')
e('Serving only the leaf works in browsers that cache intermediates and fails in `curl`, Java and mobile apps.','Some clients recover missing intermediates through caches or AIA retrieval; others do not. Serve the needed intermediate chain rather than depending on client-specific recovery.','No universalplatformfailure')
e('Certificate Transparency makes every public certificate visible, so mis-issuance is detected and bad CAs are distrusted.','CT policies promote public logging and monitoring can reveal mis-issuance; SCT promises, log operation and client enforcement do not guarantee every mis-issued certificate is immediately detected.','CTnotautomaticdetection',[ct])
Q(2,'text','Intermediates allow routine issuance while restricting root-key use; recovering from an intermediate compromise requires replacing it and ensuring relying parties reject it',0)
Q(2,'explanation','Correct. Isolation can protect the trust anchor, but revocation distribution, certificate replacement and client enforcement remain operational work.',0)
Q(3,'explanation','Correct. The existing SAN does not cover www.shop.com. Add that exact SAN or an applicable *.shop.com wildcard.',3,src=[n])
Q(4,'explanation','Correct for Let\'s Encrypt: wildcard validation uses DNS-01. Control of the challenge record or its delegation is sufficient; whole-zone administration is not inherently required.',1,src=[le])
Q(4,'workedExample','For Let\'s Encrypt, publish the DNS-01 digest derived from the challenge token and account key at _acme-challenge.apps.example.com. A DNS provider API or delegated challenge zone can automate this. Grant narrowly scoped record access where possible: the API credential may enable unauthorized certificate issuance for names it can validate. It need not grant control of the entire parent zone.',src=[a,le])
Q(5,'explanation','Correct. The proposed intermediate explicitly has CA:FALSE, so a proper path validator rejects it. Key Usage and other constraints are additional applicable checks.',2)
Q(6,'workedExample','A missing intermediate is a common explanation for this symptom, but an absent or different trust anchor can also cause it. Inspect the chain actually served (using the correct SNI hostname) and the failing client\'s trust store. Some browser/platform combinations retrieve or cache missing issuers; behavior is not universal. Configure the server to send its leaf and necessary intermediates.')
Q(7,'text','Under a soft-fail policy, inability to obtain revocation status need not reject an otherwise acceptable certificate',0)
Q(7,'explanation','Correct as a definition of soft-fail. Actual clients may use hard-fail, cached or pushed revocation data instead; there is no universal majority claim here.',0)
Q(7,'explanation','Correct. The response is signed by the issuer or an authorized responder; the client must validate signature, freshness and status. Stapling avoids that client\'s separate status request.',2)
