Q(7,'workedExample','Revocation does not alter the certificate\'s signature. Status can be obtained through CRLs, OCSP, stapling or browser-managed data, with policy-specific freshness and failure handling. Shorter validity bounds use of that certificate under a correct clock and expiry checks; it does not by itself disable the private key or prevent another certificate being issued. The cited publicTLS validity schedule reaches47days for certificates issued from15March2029.',src=[x,ca])
Q(8,'workedExample','DST Root CA X3 expired on30September2021. Clients trusting ISRG Root X1 could use a shorter path. Some OpenSSL1.0.2 configurations selected the expired chain even when an alternative existed. Older Android was a deliberate exception: it could continue using the special cross-sign path because it did not enforce expiration of that root trust anchor. Therefore old Android was not universally cut off by this expiry.',src=[dst])
Q(9,'text','Certificate Transparency monitoring can alert on an unexpected logged certificate or precertificate; enforcing browsers require acceptable SCTs',3,src=[ct])
Q(9,'explanation','Correct. An SCT is a signed promise, not proof of inclusion. CT encourages auditable issuance, and monitoring helps detection; compromised logs, policy exceptions and merge delays prevent an absolute immediate-detection guarantee.',3,src=[ct])
Q(9,'workedExample','Monitoring CT logs for your domain can reveal unexpected certificates or precertificates. Browser CT policies require acceptable SCT evidence for covered public certificates. SCTs promise later inclusion and can be delivered in several ways; log auditing and monitors are needed to make issuance visible. This detects some misuse rather than preventing every issuance.',src=[ct])
Q(10,'prompt','You run a private CA for internal mTLS. Which measures can limit accepted identities, leaf-key exposure or recovery difficulty after an issuing-intermediate compromise, assuming clients enforce the intended policy? Select all that apply.')
Q(10,'explanation','Correct for leaf-key exposure and automated recovery readiness. Short leaf lifetime alone does not stop a stolen intermediate from issuing more certificates.',1)
Q(10,'workedExample','DNS Name Constraints restrict accepted DNS identities on enforcing clients; URI/SPIFFE identities need appropriate separate treatment. Short leaf lifetimes reduce leaf-key exposure and encourage renewal automation, but do not contain a still-trusted stolen issuing key. Protecting the root preserves replacement authority; clients must actually reject the compromised intermediate. Stronger key size does not fix theft, and wider trust-store distribution increases exposure.')
